  open_questions: [
    "[RESOLVED 2026-08-03, rmw3] Not 4 competing schemes -- ONE coherent
     layered system, ratified: Layer 1 vsm_notation_spec.md A1-A11
     (domain-agnostic language canon) -> Layer 2 covenant.vsm A0/A0.1
     (VSF-specific ethics, deliberately outside the language canon,
     ∀V∈VSF not ∀V(id,L)) -> Layer 3 vos_architecture.vsm/vos_kernel_
     spec.vsm (vOS extensions; A3/A5 reuse canon's EXACT formulas,
     word-for-word verified -- canon's own A5 section already documents
     'Corollary 2 (A5⊕A2): S5(L+1).policy⊆S5(L).policy' AND the single/
     double-loop split, so covenant_ops/vos_kernel_spec/canon were never
     actually in conflict on A3/A5; new vOS-only axioms A8.vOS 'Physical
     Safety' and A12 'Mediated Execution' are legitimate namespaced
     additions, not redefinitions) -> Layer 4 covenant_ops.vsm (explicitly
     'transcribed from vos_kernel_spec.vsm §2', faithful). The apparent
     collisions were: (a) vos_architecture.vsm's OWN opening summary block
     conflated A5 with 'A5 corollary' without the qualifier the file uses
     correctly 3 other places (fixed: lines 31-36 now separate them,
     matching the file's own convention) -- likely where README's original
     bug was copied from; (b) A8.vOS's distinguishing suffix existed only
     in the formal rule() call (line 331) and a prose parenthetical, not
     consistently in every citation (fixed: all 3 references in
     vos_architecture.vsm now say 'A8.vOS'); (c) README.md's A3 comment
     mislabeled the budget/resource axiom as 'Ashby's Law' (canon: Ashby's
     Law is A6) -- fixed, comment now correctly notes 'budget/physical-
     resource ceiling (Ashby's Law is A6)'. No numbering scheme was
     collapsed or renumbered -- the fix was entirely notational
     consistency, not a substantive axiom change.",
    "[RESOLVED 2026-08-03, rmw3] A5 specifically (GS1/CT3) — ratified:
     covenant_ops.vsm's A5 (`autonomy(V) ≤ S5.ceiling; S5→V'(S5)
     @forbidden`) IS authoritative; it was never really 3 competing
     definitions. Traced to source: vos_kernel_spec.vsm §2 is A5's
     original statement (covenant_ops.vsm transcribes it faithfully).
     vos_architecture.vsm explicitly labels `S5'(V')⊆S5(V)` an 'A5
     corollary' in 3 places — README.md had dropped the word 'corollary'
     and presented the corollary AS A5 itself (real bug, now fixed:
     README.md line 33 relabeled 'A5 corollary'). CLAUDE.md's/README's
     'A5: double-loop corrections always require human approval' was an
     incomplete paraphrase of only the L3 half of covenant_ops.vsm's own
     `autonomy:` block — it omitted L2 (parameter drift, autonomous WITHIN
     the ceiling) and capability_extension (autonomous + mandatory audit).
     Both files' A5 bullets rewritten to state the full ceiling + all 3
     cases (L2/L3/capability_extension), citing covenant_ops.vsm as
     source. rmw3's own framing, confirmed accurate once terms were
     separated: the underlying justification for the L2/L3 split is A6
     (Ashby/Cyberfilter — a human cannot absorb the control loop's full
     variety), not A5 itself; A5 is the ceiling, A6 is why it's drawn
     there. 'Double-loop' is NOT sometimes-human/sometimes-not — L3 IS
     double-loop, and L3 by definition always requires human approval;
     what varies is whether a given change classifies as L2 or L3.",
    "Should covenant_ops.vsm's Ax_TraceabilityClosure be widened to
     actually enumerate A1/A4/A6/D11-D13 (GS3/GS4), or should those axioms
     be explicitly scoped OUT of the covenant's traceability claim on
     purpose (i.e. concepts.md-tier axioms are deliberately
     non-constitutional)?",
    "Does S4 need its own systems/S4_intelligence.md (GS6), and if so, who
     writes it — this touches Component A/B, markov/clima bridges, the
     ψ-channel peer question from CT6, all live/in-flux territory.",
    "Is there appetite for a single generated axiom registry (one source
     table: ID → statement → scope → defining doc → citing docs) that
     R9/docs_reconcile.py-style tooling could check against, the same
     class of fix as the config-centralization principle already
     registered for Phase C of the 2026-08-02 recovery plan? This would be
     the durable fix, not a one-time reconciliation pass.",
  ],

